          "text": "{\"target_translation\":\"I love you.\",\"dimensions\":{\"T\":{\"score\":4,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.99,\"reason\":\"The flower directly avows her present loving state and explicitly says that the prince had not known of it because of her own fault.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"target\",\"quotation\":\"je t'aime\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Mais oui, je t'aime, lui dit la fleur. Tu n'en a rien su, par ma faute.\"}]},\"P\":{\"score\":0,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.96,\"reason\":\"The expression avows love but does not itself promise future conduct, assume an obligation, or propose a relational undertaking. The neighbouring wishes and instructions should not be transferred onto it.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"target\",\"quotation\":\"je t'aime\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Tâche d'être heureux...Laisse ce globe tranquille. Je n'en veux plus.\"}]},\"E\":{\"score\":0,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.9,\"reason\":\"Although the departure is emotionally charged, the avowal is presented within the flower's controlled, calm speech rather than as a reflex-like or uncontrolled discharge of affect.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Il ne comprenait pas cette douceur calme.\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Car elle ne voulait pas qu'il la vît pleurer.\"}]},\"O\":{\"score\":0,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.96,\"reason\":\"The core force of the target is adequately represented as an avowal of a loving state; no additional core function beyond T/P/E is evident in the expression itself.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"target\",\"quotation\":\"je t'aime\"}]}},\"original_speech_situation\":\"At the little prince's departure, after apologising and telling him to try to be happy, the flower calmly tells him that she loves him and that her own fault prevented him from knowing it.\",\"affection_evidence\":\"present\",\"undertaking_and_uptake\":\"The target itself contains no undertaking. In the surrounding turn, the flower wishes him happiness and relinquishes his protective care, but those are separate acts. He does not explicitly accept or reject the love avowal; he responds by raising concerns about wind and animals.\",\"recognised_work\":true,\"recognition_note\":\"The supplied chapter headings, characters, and wording are recognisable as Antoine de Saint-Exupéry's Le Petit Prince. The classification relies only on the supplied passages.\",\"limitations\":\"The passages strongly support the represented avowal and affection, but they do not provide an explicit verbal uptake of the avowal by the little prince or establish any commitment terms attached to it.\"}"
        }
